  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\n- Dry weather through most of Sat with slowly increasing temps\n\nThe upper trough that brought the cold front through the area \nyesterday remains over the area this morning. However the trough \naxis has shifted east of the area, putting the area under confluent \nflow aloft. This is leading to increasing subsidence and high \npressure in the lower levels. This pattern holds through Friday, and \nonly slowly breaks down on Saturday.\n\nTemperatures aloft at 850 mb have dropped down to around +7C behind \nthe cold front and under the cold pool aloft. These temperatures \naloft will support max temps in the mid 70s today. We will see them \nslowly recover through Saturday when the 850 mb temps warm to around \n+16-17C, helping to bring warm air up near 90F back to the area. Dew \npoints will slowly creep up over the area during this time frame \nalso. \n\n- Next chances of rain Sat night through Mon\n\nThe confluent flow aloft, and sfc ridging will move sufficiently out \nof the area by Saturday night. This will allow a short wave to spill \nover the large scale ridge axis, and additional short wave activity \nwill be possible.\n\nEven though the short wave activity will pick up next Sunday and \nMonday, low level moisture return is somewhat limited. The low level \nflow ahead of the short wave is not directly from the Gulf. \nEvapotranspiration from the corn fields in Iowa and Illinois will \nhelp increase the moisture some. This has the potential to be rounds \nof storms affecting the area as these waves will be riding the heat \ndome to our west.",
